/*
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: geolocation=(), camera=(), microphone=(), payment=(), interest-cohort=()
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; base-uri 'self'; form-action 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'; object-src 'none'; script-src 'self'; connect-src 'self' https://api.vegasthisweekend.com; img-src 'self' data: https://media.vegasthisweekend.com; media-src 'self' https://media.vegasthisweekend.com; font-src 'self'; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; manifest-src 'self'; upgrade-insecure-requests

# CSP notes:
#
# manifest-src is stated rather than left to the default-src fallback. The fallback does
# cover it — the manifest is same-origin — but a blocked manifest fails the way every
# other CSP problem on this project has failed: the page looks perfect, the install
# prompt simply never appears, and nothing in the repo hints at why.
#
# media-src is REQUIRED for event videos and its absence is not obvious: with no media-src
# the browser falls back to default-src 'self' and silently refuses every off-origin video,
# so the player looks broken while the markup is perfect. It now names the R2 domain only,
# which is where every event video lives — including TikTok posts, which the admin panel
# downloads and mirrors rather than linking (see app/video_sources.py).
#
# There is no longer an inline-script hash or a plausible.io origin here: analytics is
# first-party, so the page loads no third-party script at all. If you ever add an inline
# script back, the browser console prints the sha256 to add — it is not guessable.
#
# connect-src must keep allowing the API origin: interaction counts POST to
# /interactions there, and a missing entry would show up as analytics quietly recording
# nothing rather than as an error anyone notices.
#
# script-src is 'self' with nothing else, and that is the point: the deployed page loads
# no third-party script at all. The two Cloudflare Web Analytics entries that used to be
# here (static.cloudflareinsights.com in script-src, cloudflareinsights.com in
# connect-src) are deliberately gone. That beacon is injected at the edge, so it never
# appeared in this repo's HTML — an "it isn't in the source" check would not have found
# it, and it only showed up as a console violation on the deployed site.
#
# Removing it from the CSP is the enforcement, not the configuration: Web Analytics must
# ALSO be switched off in the Cloudflare dashboard, or the beacon keeps being injected and
# every visitor's browser prints a violation instead of quietly not being tracked. With
# both done, no visitor's page ever contacts Cloudflare's analytics endpoints, and traffic
# numbers come from the API's own server-side counters instead — which never see anything
# beyond what the request already told us.
#
# connect-src listed the raw railway.app origin as well during the cutover to the custom
# API domain, because the CSP ships in this repo while VITE_API_BASE_URL lives in Netlify's
# settings — the two cannot change in the same instant. The deployed bundle was confirmed
# calling api.vegasthisweekend.com only, so that entry is gone.
#
# Getting any of this wrong is unusually unpleasant to debug: a blocked request surfaces as
# a CSP violation in the console, not a network error, so the API looks healthy while the
# site looks broken. If you point the frontend at a new API origin, change it here in the
# same commit.
#
# img-src and media-src name media.vegasthisweekend.com and nothing else, which means the
# app now contacts no third-party host at all: no cookies, no PII, and no venue CDN
# learning a visitor's IP address and which event they were looking at. That was the whole
# point of mirroring into R2, and this line is the half that enforces it — the mirroring
# alone only makes third-party URLs rare, not impossible.
#
# Tightened on 31 Jul 2026, after confirming all 14 live events served both fields from
# the R2 domain. Do not widen either back to `https:` to make one stubborn URL work: an
# unmirrored image now fails to load and falls back to the locally generated poster, which
# is the correct outcome. If a whole page of cards goes blank, something stopped mirroring
# — check the admin panel's media warning rather than reaching for this line.

# Netlify serves .webmanifest as application/octet-stream, and this site sends
# X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff — so the browser will not sniff its way to the right
# type and simply refuses the manifest. The page looks perfect and the install prompt
# never appears, with nothing logged anywhere obvious. Found by checking the deployed
# headers; a local build cannot show it, because there are no headers in a build.
/manifest.webmanifest
  Content-Type: application/manifest+json
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600

# The icon set changes only when the branding does.
/icons/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800

# Hashed build assets are immutable; the HTML shell must never be cached.
/assets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/index.html
  Cache-Control: no-cache
